  {"signature": "Sort u",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "inductive",
   "id": "PUnit",
   "docstring":
   "The canonical universe-polymorphic type with just one element.\n\nIt should be used in contexts that require a type to be universe polymorphic, thus disallowing\n`Unit`.\n"},
  {"signature": "Sort u → Sort v → Sort (max (max 1 u) v)",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "inductive",
   "id": "PProd",
   "docstring":
   "A product type in which the types may be propositions, usually written `α ×' β`.\n\nThis type is primarily used internally and as an implementation detail of proof automation. It is\nrarely useful in hand-written code.\n"},
  {"signature": "{α : Sort u} → {β : Sort v} → α → β → α ×' β",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "constructor",
   "id": "PProd.mk",
   "docstring": ""},
  {"signature": "ℕ",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "constructor",
   "id": "Nat.zero",
   "docstring":
   "Zero, the smallest natural number.\n\nUsing `Nat.zero` explicitly should usually be avoided in favor of the literal `0`, which is the\n[simp normal form](lean-manual://section/simp-normal-forms).\n"},
  {"signature": "PUnit.{u}",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "constructor",
   "id": "PUnit.unit",
   "docstring": "The only element of the universe-polymorphic unit type. "},
  {"signature":
   "{motive : ℕ → Sort u} → (t : ℕ) → motive Nat.zero → ((n : ℕ) → motive n.succ) → motive t",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "definition",
   "id": "Nat.casesOn",
   "docstring": ""},
  {"signature": "Type u → ℕ → Type u",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "inductive",
   "id": "OfNat",
   "docstring":
   "The class `OfNat α n` powers the numeric literal parser. If you write\n`37 : α`, Lean will attempt to synthesize `OfNat α 37`, and will generate\nthe term `(OfNat.ofNat 37 : α)`.\n\nThere is a bit of infinite regress here since the desugaring apparently\nstill contains a literal `37` in it. The type of expressions contains a\nprimitive constructor for \"raw natural number literals\", which you can directly\naccess using the macro `nat_lit 37`. Raw number literals are always of type `Nat`.\nSo it would be more correct to say that Lean looks for an instance of\n`OfNat α (nat_lit 37)`, and it generates the term `(OfNat.ofNat (nat_lit 37) : α)`.\n"},
  {"signature": "{α : Type u} → {x : ℕ} → α → OfNat α x",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "constructor",
   "id": "OfNat.mk",
   "docstring": ""},
  {"signature":
   "{α : Sort u_1} →\n  {a : α} → {motive : (a_1 : α) → a = a_1 → Sort u} → motive a ⋯ → {a_1 : α} → (t : a = a_1) → motive a_1 t",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "recursor",
   "id": "Eq.rec",
   "docstring": ""},
  {"signature": "∀ {α : Sort u} {a : α}, a = a",
   "module": "Init.Prelude",
   "kind": "definition",
   "id": "rfl",
   "docstring":
   "`rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the\nsame as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.\n\nThis is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although\nthe statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is\ndefinitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in\nLean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality.\n"},
  {"signature": "∀ (x : ℕ) (f : Nat.below x), 0 + x = x",
   "module": "Init.Data.Nat.Basic",
   "kind": "definition",
   "id": "Nat.zero_add._f",
   "docstring": ""},
